---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/paths';

// For organizations that want to map their own work to the library: why, what they get, and how to start.
const kitRepo = 'https://github.com/enterprise-blueprints/blueprint-kit';
const sample = 'https://enterprise-blueprints.github.io/blueprint-kit/';
---
<Base title="Blueprint Kit" description="Map your organization's solutions, services, and products to Enterprise Blueprints, and publish them on a site of your own.">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <header class="page-header">
      <p class="kicker">For organizations</p>
      <h1>The Blueprint Kit</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Map your organization's solutions, services, and products to the library's missions, capabilities,
        and patterns, and publish them on a site of your own.
      </p>
      <p class="kit-actions">
        <a class="button button--primary" href={kitRepo} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Get the kit on GitHub<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>
        <a class="button" href={sample} target="_blank" rel="noopener">See a sample site<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Why use it</h2>
      <p>
        Enterprise Blueprints describes what public organizations are responsible for and what that work
        depends on, in shared terms: <a href={href('missions/')}>missions</a>, the outcomes they seek,
        the <a href={href('capabilities/')}>capabilities</a> and requirements behind them, and
        <a href={href('patterns/')}>example patterns</a> that might realize them. The library stays free of
        anyone's products, so everyone can use it as common ground.
      </p>
      <p>
        The kit is for any organization that serves those missions, such as an agency's own technology
        team, a firm, a nonprofit, or a university, to say where its work fits, in those same terms. People
        planning a mission can then find what helps with each part of it, described the same way.
      </p>

      <h2>What you get</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A site of your own that works like this one,</strong> with the mission explorer and the
          outcome, capability, and pattern pages, in your name and color.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The whole library, or just the missions your work builds on,</strong> with your offerings shown
          beside the library's items they help with. A small book marks what comes from the library, and each
          of those pages links back here.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Missions, capabilities, and patterns of your own,</strong> if the library doesn't cover
          them yet, built from the library's shared ones.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Packages in the library's format, with a catalog,</strong> so tools such as Solution Studio
          read yours beside the library's.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What goes in it</h2>
      <p>
        Mostly resources: a service you offer, a product or one of its features, a demo, a template, training,
        or a case study. Each one names the missions, outcomes, capabilities, requirements, or patterns it
        helps with, by the IDs shown on this site's pages, such as <code>eb:capability:records-management</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        A product goes on one of a pattern's ways to source it, such as "configure a product", rather than
        becoming a pattern of its own. That keeps the library's patterns examples, with yours beside them.
      </p>

      <h2>How to start</h2>
      <ol class="kit-steps">
        <li>
          On GitHub, open the <a href={kitRepo} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Blueprint Kit<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>
          and choose <strong>Use this template</strong> to make a repository of your own. You need Node 22.12
          or later and pnpm.
        </li>
        <li>Name your site and choose its color in <code>kit.yaml</code>.</li>
        <li>
          Describe your offerings in <code>source/offerings/resources.yaml</code>, each with what it helps with.
          The sample has one of each kind to start from.
        </li>
        <li>
          Run <code>pnpm content:build</code> to build your packages, and <code>pnpm verify</code> to check
          every link against the library.
        </li>
        <li>
          Push to GitHub with Pages turned on, and the kit's workflow publishes your site and its catalog. Or
          run <code>pnpm content:publish</code> and host the built site anywhere you like.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>The kit's README walks through each step, and its sample site shows the result.</p>

      <h2>Staying current</h2>
      <p>
        Your site builds on one library release, so it changes only when you choose. Each week, the kit opens
        a pull request in your repository that moves to the library's current release and the kit's latest
        pages, with the result of its checks, for you to review and merge.
      </p>

      <h2>Yours to keep</h2>
      <p>
        Your packages carry your own license and live in your own repository; the library never includes
        them. To keep them to your team, host your site where only your team can reach it, or share the
        package files, which Solution Studio can open directly.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .kit-actions { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .6rem; margin: 1.25rem 0 0; }
  .kit-steps { max-width: 54rem; padding-left: 1.4rem; margin: 0 0 1em; }
  .kit-steps li { margin-bottom: .5em; padding-left: .25rem; }
</style>
